{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1108212-age-to-embrace-neonates-vs-elders","id":1108212,"title":"Age to Embrace -- Neonates vs. Elders","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-26T18:50:54-07:00","id":1108212,"title":"Age to Embrace -- Neonates vs. Elders","content":"In your setting, what would you say is the typical age for a first Embrace? A couple of decades? A century?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-26T19:00:04-07:00","id":1108215,"title":"","content":"You mean when you do your first Embrace?<br /> <br /> Not counting accidents, there's probably a lot of variation. The more bound by rules an area or group is, the longer you would be likely to wait. And the less structured a place, the more likely it's going to happen, especially since having a fledgling neonate can help you establish or reaffirm your own structure. Roving things like the Oberlochs seem practically Embrace-happy, at least among the bosses, and I think one every five years is probably a fair bet depending on circumstance, while the Morbius, a rather zealous bloodline attached to the Lancea et Sanctum, seem to hold the matter pretty sacred and I think one every century might even be pushing it. It'd be hard to put an 'age' on that kind of thing, since it would depend so much on the individual personality of the vampire and their circumstances. I suppose at the least it would be natural, in America and Western Europe, to think that an elder vampire has Embraced at least once.<br /> <br /> Then you always get exceptions. Someone who has fallen in love and must have their paramour stand with them through eternity. A bloodline that was decimated and, for some reason or another, simply needs more right now. One vampire might Embrace someone solely to 'get at' another vampire who was close to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-26-2017, 10:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-26T19:19:23-07:00","id":1108225,"title":"","content":"I feel like the Embrace is such that each one is relatively unique. Some vampires Embrace shortly after they've been Embraced, others do it after decades or centuries. So I treat it on a case by case basis, rather than a general trend.<br /> <br /> I agree with nofather about being bound by rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-27T10:03:45-07:00","id":1108438,"title":"","content":"With 1 exception in VtM, I don't recall seeing a PC sire a Childe. I assumed other groups had the experience, so I was curious. I've put together several settings, though, and am fairly anal about naming and aging 90% of the kindred in the locale.<br /> <br /> In my brand new game, we are using the house rule that the Embrace costs a dot of Blood Potency and a dot of Willpower. It makes for some interesting dynamics, and a serious decrease in the number of vampires running around. Someone with only 1 BP is liable to create a revenant. Of the percentage of vampires who survive half a century, how many are willing to weaken their innate power so much to create who will likely be an eventual rival? Likewise, the various ancilla in a city have to weigh the benefits of a Childe vs. being slightly weaker than the rival ancilla. An Elder may want to sire a Childe to thin the blood for another half-century, but even he likely won't empty his hard-saved vitae capital to sire a brood. Thus, in the setting I'm running in, you have 6 100-300 year old vampires, half a dozen ancilla, and a few neonates (the PCs).<br /> <br /> When I proposed the house rule, I could see how it would change the elder dynamic, but only in implementing it in a new setting did the society age structure become apparent to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2203,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","user":"Corrigan","date":". 06-27-2017, 01:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}